<div class="page-header">
  <h1 class="page-title">Cookie policy</h1>
</div>

<div class="page-prose">
  <p>
    This is a static site: it has no server of its own, it stores nothing about you and it
    <strong>sets no cookies by itself</strong>.
  </p>

  <p>
    To know how many visits it gets we use <a href="https://umami.is/" rel="noopener">Umami</a>,
    a statistics tool that <strong>sets no cookies and stores no personal data</strong>: it counts
    visits in aggregate and anonymously (which pages are seen, from which country and with which
    browser), without following you from one visit to the next or across sites.
  </p>

  <p>
    A few pages do embed third-party content, and those services may set their own cookies when
    you load the page containing them:
  </p>

  <ul class="page-list">
    <li>
      Four articles include a <strong>YouTube</strong> video (Google):
      <a href="/en/articles/sabiote-castle-i">Sabiote Castle I</a>,
      <a href="/en/articles/castillo-de-la-iruela">El Castillo de La Iruela</a>,
      <a href="/en/articles/iglesia-de-santo-domingo">Iglesia de Santo Domingo</a> and
      <a href="/en/articles/mina-fundicion-de-la-tortilla">Mina-Fundición de la Tortilla</a>.
      They load in its privacy-enhanced mode, which sets no cookies until you press play.
    </li>
    <li>
      The pages of <a href="/en/the-books/the-first-book">the first book</a> and
      <a href="/en/the-books/the-second-book">the second</a> include their presentations hosted
      on <strong>Speakerdeck</strong>.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Those cookies are set by those services, not by us, and are governed by their own policies. If
    you would rather avoid them, you can block third-party cookies in your browser settings: the
    rest of the site works exactly the same.
  </p>
</div>
